text:
Mr. Carse looks at the importance of the euro for Hong Kong Speech by the Deputy Chief Executive of the Hong Kong Monetary Authority, Mr. David Carse, at a Semi on the euro, held in Hong Kong on 9/11/98.

## Introduction

Ladies and gentlemen,

I am very pleased to be here this morning to speak at this seminar on th organized by the Vocational Training Council in conjunction with the British Consulateand the Hong Kong Institute of Bankers. With the introduction of the euro less than tw away, this is obviously a good time to hold such a seminar. It is also fitting to hol Kong where our fixed exchange rate system gives us a natural sympathy for its logical e the single currency. We are fortunate to have a number of experts on the euro here tod us the benefit of their views, including my former colleague from the Bank of Englan Townend.

My role in the seminar is to say a few words on the importance of the eu Hong Kong. I will also take the opportunity to brief you on some of the regulatory st we have been taking to prepare for the euro.

The introduction of the euro is a truly momentous change in the global eco and financial landscape. It is not simply a change of currency for the participatin though that in itself is a major event. Rather, it is a crucial step in the economic integration of the European Union ('EU') countries and the creation of the single mar change will involve 300 million consumers, 16% of world GDP and 29% of world trade. demonstrates why the euro is an important event for Hong Kong. As the UK Chancellor o Exchequer recently made clear, we are now living in a global economy, in which each ec can directly affect the prospects of every other. Where the economy concerned is the s EU, its potential to affect other economies - for good or bad - is without question.

This is reinforced by the existing trade and financial links between Hong and the EU countries:

- the EU ranks 3rd among Hong Kong's trading partners in the world;
- in the first eight months of this year, the EU accounted for HK$245 bil 13.2%  of  Hong  Kong's  total  trade  -  much  of  this  represents  entrepot between the EU and mainland China;
- the  EU  is  the  largest  external  investor  in  Hong  Kong,  accounting  for HK$200 billion or 34% of the total;
- more  than  700  EU  companies  use  Hong  Kong  as  regional  headquarters  or regional offices;
- the EU is the 2nd largest source of visitors and 4th largest source of earnings for Hong Kong;
- there  are  over  50  licensed  banks  and  other  authorized  institutions  in Kong from the EU countries, the largest concentration in Asia.

Clearly, what happens in the EU matters to Hong Kong. What therefore will the impact of the euro on the participating countries? There should certainly be a economic benefits, including in particular:

- a huge increase in transparency from the single currency which will ma easier to compare prices across national frontiers. This will boost comp bring down costs and prices, and improve efficiency;
- a reduction in transaction costs, in particular from the elimination of exchange commissions. This will encourage trade and cross-border investm flows;
- lower  interest  rates  in  individual  countries  from  the  elimination  fr foreign exchange risk premium. This will reduce financing costs and stim investment and output;
- the development of larger and more liquid debt and stock markets across euro  zone.  This  will  create  a  vast  new  pool  of  capital  for  companies giving a further stimulus to investment;
- tighter  fiscal  discipline  on  the  member  countries  under  the  Stabili Growth Pact and monetary discipline exercised by the new European Centr Bank.  Both  these  should  ease  inflationary  expectations  and  produce  a  m stable financial environment for the euro zone.

In summary, the single currency should be able to deliver higher economic g and therefore increased demand for imports by the euro countries. As one of the EU' trading partners, this should benefit Hong Kong. Hong Kong may also be able to take ad of the increased flow of funds for global investment which may be forthcoming from a enlarged  European  capital  market.  Furthermore,  to  the  extent  that  an  offshore  euro develops, Hong Kong would be an obvious location for this in Asia. This reflects the large presence of European banks in Hong Kong and our role as a bridge between Europe mainland China. Finally, Hong Kong is one of the world's largest holders of foreign e reserves and the euro will provide interesting opportunities for diversification of our

Of course, the advent of the single currency also brings with it certain r discipline imposed by the single currency will not always be comfortable, particularly business cycles diverge in different member countries. This could set up tensions which worst lead to a break-up of monetary union or at least undermine confidence in the e current  dispute  between  politicians  and  the  European  Central  Bank  over  interest  rat might be a foretaste of what is to come. A break-up of monetary union seems highly unli the potential for internal wrangling is clearly there and that could be disruptive to w markets.

There must also be at least a possibility that closer integration of the make it more self-contained and inward-looking. Intra-EU trade could increase, while t the rest of the world and Hong Kong could diminish. Moreover, will the enlarged EU c market suck in capital from Asia to Europe? In the first four months of this year, fo

Japanese net purchases of European securities totalled US$9.9 billion, while net sale and  stocks  in  Asia  totalled  US$2.1  billion.  Extended  diversion  of  capital  to  Europ undermine Asia's growth prospects as well as Hong Kong's position as an international f centre. This could also happen if the European banks are tempted to consolidate their b Asia to focus their efforts on their enlarged home market.

This is not the occasion to be pessimistic. Overall, I believe that the eur good thing for Europe and for the world economy. But as with any major change in the financial  architecture,  Hong  Kong  will  need  to  monitor  developments  closely  and  re quickly to emerging threats as well as opportunities.

Let me now turn to the HKMA's regulatory response to the euro in Hong Kon First, banks in Hong Kong need to be prepared to handle and account for the euro, par during the awkward transitional period before the legacy currencies disappear entirely. not ready, many of the potential benefits of the euro for Hong Kong will simply not ma We have therefore issued advice to the banks on the steps that they need to take, such of business strategies and business processes that might be affected by the Euro; revie accounts in the legacy currencies; adaptation of information systems; and review of con might be affected by introduction of the euro.

Secondly, we have thought it sensible to introduce legislation that will pr continuity of contracts in relation to the introduction of the euro. In doing so, we the example of the EU itself as well as some other jurisdictions. In theory, such legi be  unnecessary  since  the  well-established  'lex  monetae'  or  'law  of  the  currency'  p means that the currency of a payment obligation should be determined by the law of the of that currency. This means for example that a payment obligation payable in French would become payable in euros after the introduction of the euro because that is the si applies under the law of France.

However, despite this general principle, concerns have been expressed that to contracts might argue that the introduction of the euro represented a fundamental circumstances, bringing the contract to an end or requiring its terms to be adjusted. another feature of the introduction of the euro is that it will replace the ECU at a r one on 1 January 1999. The ECU is a currency basket but not a currency in its own ri 'lex monetae' principle does not apply in this case and therefore the euro will not au substitute for the ECU in contracts.

It is to remove uncertainty on these points that we have decided to legisl believe  that  this  is  appropriate  for  an  international  financial  centre  such  as  Hong financial and legal community in Hong Kong strongly supports this view.

This  is  just  one  specific  example  of  how  Hong  Kong  is  affected  by introduction of the euro. There will undoubtedly be more far-reaching effects on our and our financial system as the single currency takes root. This is why this seminar is important. I hope that you will find it enjoyable and useful.

## Mr. Stals asks whether the East Asian financial crisis can be repeated in Sou

Africa Address by the Governor of the Reserve Bank of South Africa, Mr. C. Stals, at a b meeting organised by Life Line West Rand in Krugersdorp on 29/4/98.

## 1. Background to the East Asian crisis

It is now history that the economies of a number of East Asian countries colla the second half of 1997 and created disruptive turmoil in financial markets around the w countries  most  affected  were  Indonesia,  the  Republic  of  Korea,  Malaysia,  the  Philippi Thailand.

A  number  of  reasons  were  provided  for  this  collapse  which  shocked  the  wo economy,  particularly  because  it  occurred  in  those  countries  that  were  the  most  succ achieving  their  economic  development  objectives  during  the  past  thirty  years.  The  main included:

- a substantial build-up of foreign liabilities -- particularly of a short-te took place over the protracted period of rapid economic growth;
- the financial sectors were not properly supervised and regulated, with the that banking institutions accumulated large amounts of non-performing loans provided for in capital and reserves;
- there were unjustified governmental interventions in the normal working o financial  markets.  Financial  institutions  were,  for  example,  instructed  t loans to preferred clients at low interest rates;
- governance within private sector institutions, public corporations and gove departments was of low quality and, in the case of some countries, undermine corruption;
- macroeconomic  policies  did  not  adjust  to  the  changing  environment;  marke were liberalised without concurrent adjustment in internal economic struc money  supply  and  bank  credit  extension  increased  almost  unchecked;  inter rates were kept artificially low, and exchange rates were not allowed to a accordance with changes in the overall balance of payments situation.

The  final  collapse  was  triggered  first  in  Thailand,  when  foreign  investo confidence in the economy and started to withdraw part of their more liquid investments country. The authorities were very slow to react to the threatening crisis. As became evide stage, important information on the deteriorating economic situation was withheld from the and vital statistics were, in some cases, deliberately not released.

In  the  end,  the  International  Monetary  Fund,  the  World  Bank,  a  number  of governments of industrial countries, and the international investment community, had to p massive amount of financial support -- more than US $120 billion -- to assist these coun countries themselves also had to implement painful corrective measures that will, over tim equilibrium in their economies. These programmes inter alia provide for:

- relatively restrictive monetary policies that must prevent further depreci the currencies to protect domestic institutions with outstanding foreign l from further losses, and must eventually reduce inflation;

- the restructuring of the financial sectors through predetermined reform age each country. Public sector rescue operations must be supported by cost sh sacrifices  from  the  private  sector,  and  prudential  regulations  for  f institutions must be tightened;
- improvements  in  public  and  corporate  governance  and  a  strengthening transparency and accountability;
- a reduction in the dependence of public sector budgets on external funds fiscal provision for the costs of restructuring and recapitalising banking s

The devastating effects of the crises can be illustrated by a few statistics fr the affected countries. According to a recent World Bank report, prices of key commodities rice, increased by 25 per cent or more in Indonesia, leading to food riots and major demo against the Government. Unemployment is rising sharply and will increase the number of living below the poverty line from 23 million to 40 million during the course of 1998.

In Korea, bankruptcies and unemployment are on the rise. Eight of the thirty conglomerates  (chaebol)  have  filed  for  liquidation.  Unemployment  is  expected  to  increas 500,000 last year to 1.2 million workers soon.

In Thailand, the slump in industry is severe. Monthly sales of motor vehicles consumer electronic goods and durable consumer goods are now running at levels of 35 to cent down on last year. Industrial output growth is expected to decline by about 50 per cen

In terms of the latest World Economic Outlook of the International Monetary F the global economic growth rate for 1998 will be reduced by the East Asian crisis from a forecast of 3½ per cent to not more than 3 per cent in 1998. This will have an adverse effe trade, and also on the export prospects for countries such as South Africa. It further de outlook for an early economic recovery in Japan, and also holds adverse effects for many market economies.

## 2. The South African situation

The South African economy was so far relatively little affected by the East crisis.  The  foreign  exchange  market,  and  particularly  the  exchange  rate  of  the  rand,  c pressure for only a few weeks in October last year. Prices on the Johannesburg Stock Exchan more directly affected, although the declines registered at that time were wiped out by a run over the past three months.

Some other emerging markets, for example Brazil, were affected much more sever than South Africa. The question is often asked why was South Africa left almost unimpaired East Asian crisis? There are a number of reasons for this:

Firstly,  it  must  be  remembered  that  South  Africa  had  its  own  mini-crisis  i when sudden capital outflows from the country forced a depreciation of the r more than 20 per cent, and the introduction of restrictive fiscal and monetary that reduced the growth rate in total domestic expenditure from 5.0 per cent to 2.7 per cent in 1996, and 1.4 per cent in 1997. At the time of the East Asi South Africa had just gone through a very painful downward economic adjustme

period that satisfied foreign investors of our resolve to macroeconomic disciplines.

apply acc

Secondly,  the  South  African  problems  of  1996  were  created  by  a  cycli disequilibrium, following from excessive rates of growth in total dom expenditure, and this situation could be corrected fairly easily with the rig monetary and fiscal policies. In most of the East Asian countries, the fund problems are more of a structural nature and will require longer and more p adjustments to restore growth to previous levels.

There are, nevertheless, a number of lessons to be learned by South Africa fr East Asian crisis. Our country is also being integrated more and more into the global mark world and, as in the case of those countries, developments in South Africa are also subject critical surveillance of the international investors' community. At this stage, their cri are being focused on:

- the health and soundness of the domestic financial sector;
- the openness and efficiency of the money and capital markets, and the mark foreign exchange;
- the transparency, both of policies and of macroeconomic data made availabl the authorities;
- the  quality  of  governance  in  both  the  public  and  the  private  sectors economy;
- the macroeconomic policies followed in the country by the monetary and fi authorities, and in respect of labour and commodity markets;
- the overall balance of payments situation, including developments in the c and in the capital accounts; and
- the total outstanding foreign liabilities, with special reference to the vu of the country for sudden outflows of capital.

In recent months, foreign investors showed their satisfaction with the South situation by continuing to increase their investments in South Africa. Over the first fou this year, non-residents increased their holdings of South African equities and bonds by n R34  billion.  This  enabled  South  Africa  to  continue  with  the  programme  of  gradually  r exchange controls, and also helped the Reserve Bank to increase its net foreign reserves billion during the first quarter of this year.

South Africa can continue to hold the confidence of foreign investors, provi are also prepared to continue to apply the sound monetary and fiscal policies of the past foreign market sentiment towards the country can change very quickly, and we can easily be into a new financial market crisis of our own.

With  his  latest  Budget,  the  Minister  of  Finance  reconfirmed  the  Governmen determination to maintain the necessary disciplines in fiscal policy. The Reserve Bank is its  conservative  approach  towards  monetary  policy,  and  is  equally  determined  to  maintai disciplines in the management of the money and banking systems of the country. In this way Africa will continue to benefit from being part of the global financial system, and to mak savings of other countries to help finance growth and development.

In summary, it depends on us and on the economic policies we apply, whether an Asian crisis will also develop in South Africa. Foreign investors nodded their approval of

of  the  past  year.  We  should  therefore  not  deviate  unnecessarily  from  the  course  of  th policies.
